  "strategy": "1. **Approach and Rationale**  \nThe central idea is to model the step function \\( h \\) as a sparse mixture of shifted bump functions optimally placed in the interval \\([0, 2]\\). Unlike the previous continuous optimization, this approach generates candidate solutions through combinatorial search. It leverages the observation that optimal step functions have localized \"activity periods\" with minimal cross-overlap. By probabilistically generating and mutating candidate solutions with deliberate structural properties (avoiding periodic artifacts, ensuring even coverage), we aim to break local optima. This method is more robust to discrete constraints and grid resolution issues, makes fewer statistical assumptions, and explicitly handles feasibility during evolution.\n\n**Assumptions/Rationale**:  \n- The minimal \\( C \\) is achieved by step functions with constant intervals, which simplifies optimization despite the broader space being nonconvex.  \n- Substantial overlap reduction requires the candidate function to cover \\([0, 2]\\) evenly while keeping its shifts sparse.  \n**Limitations**:  \n- Requires discrete space exploration at high resolution (\u2264800 points).  \n- Combinatorial swaps may be slow; trade-offs between resolution and flips controlled by `n` and `flip_budget`.\n\n2. **Mathematical Specification**  \nWe represent \\( h \\) solutions as binary vectors of length \\( n \\) (resolutions from 24 to 800) with exactly \\( n/2 \\) ones.  \n**Variables/Representation**:  \n- \\( h \\in \\{0,1\\}^n \\), ensuring \\( \\sum h[i] \\Delta x = 1 \\).  \n- \\( \\Delta x = 2.0 / n \\), so \\( \\sum h[i] = n/2 \\).  \n**Objective**:  \nMinimize \\( C_5 = \\max_{k \\in [-2, 2]} \\left( \\Delta x \\cdot \\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} h[i] \\cdot (1 - h[(i + \\lfloor k/\\Delta x \\rfloor)_n]) \\right) \\), approximated via grid shifts (mode='full' in `np.correlate`).  \n**Constraints**: Binary outputs (\\( 0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1 \\)) and exact mass (n/2 ones).  \n**Structural Fitness**: Candidates are filtered by:  \n- Avoiding \"dense\" regions (no more than \\( m \\) consecutive ones at any point).  \n- Preventing periodic patterns by ensuring that autocorrelation at small lags is minimized.\n\n3. **Implementation Procedure**  \n**Stage 1: Input Handling**  \n- Initialize \\( \\Delta x \\) and \\( n \\) (start small, e.g., 128).  \n- Use provided parent vector (\\( initial\\_h\\_values \\)) if available, else uniform initialization.  \n**Stage 2: Candidate Generation**  \n- **Structure-Driven Initialization**:\n  - Uniform/periodic version for fallback.\n  - Sparse version: Randomly place ones such that they are evenly spaced (\\( n/2 \\) points over \\([0, 2]\\) in grid-aligned coordinates).  \n- **Mutation Guideline**:\n  - Swap one-zero pairs biased toward the least-overlapping regions (identified by current correlation peak).  \n  - Restrict consecutive ones or zeros beyond a threshold (controlled by `density_clamp`).  \n  - Bounds \\( 0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1 \\) are inherent.  \n**Stage 3: Optimization Loop**  \n- **Lower-level objective evaluation** restricts to shift windows \\( |k| \\leq 4\\Delta x \\) to avoid grid saturation issues.  \n- compute_c5 revised: max over grid shifts (check tolerance: \\( |\\text{new}_C - \\text{old}_C| < 1e-6 \\)).\n- During mutation, enforce: no more than `max_ones` consecutive ones (20-40, depending on resolution).  \n- **Feasibility Check**: Always ensures \\( \\sum h[i] = n/2 \\).  \n**Stage 4: Resolution Control**  \n- Upward phases (n=128, 256, 400, ...) with adaptive learning rate above 0.7 tolerance improvement.  \n- Downward if slow improvement; `n * 0.5` if stuck.\n\n4. **Search Choices & Variations**  \n- **Default Configuration**:\n  - n \u2208 [312, 400, 600, 800].\n  - max_ones = 30 (clamped).\n  - min_flips = 5, max_flips = 100 per iteration.\n  - Temperature schedule: step factor from 0.5-2.0 based on fitness improvement.\n  - **Density Clamping**: If >5% clamped, reduce density by 15% per run.\n- **Variations Involved**:\n  - Add random spacing noise (jitter) to generated sparse starts.\n  - Random low-pass filtering (alternate to clamping).\n\n5. **Compute Budget & Robustness**  \n- Time: ~30s per resolution at n=800; limit resolution jumps if >90% budget spent.  \n- Heuristic: If runs stall and cdf >0.9 (past best), switch to a new variation.  \n- Worker Functions:\n  - `compute_c5` modified: clip lags to [-4,4] in grid terms (indices -n//4 to n//4).\n\n6. **Validation & Return Contract**  \n- Ensure \\( 0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1 \\), \\( \\sum h[i] = n/2 \\), finite `C_5`, and pass evaluator.  \n- Checks:  \n  - End of loop: Recalc `C_5` to confirm improvement.  \n  - Output fallback parents if time expires.",
